WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.810 --> 00:00:04.890
Black Mirror's fourth season hasn't even aired
yet, but fans are already starting to parse

00:00:04.890 --> 00:00:06.540
out Easter eggs from the preview.

00:00:06.700 --> 00:00:10.060
And if it's anything like the seasons before
it, the new season should be filled with them.

00:00:10.200 --> 00:00:14.200
But before we get to the new season, let's
take a look back at some of the most interesting

00:00:14.200 --> 00:00:16.000
references Black Mirror has made so far.

00:00:16.620 --> 00:00:17.860
The Waldo Moment

00:00:18.560 --> 00:00:22.820
Black Mirror Easter eggs first started appearing
in Season 2's "The Waldo Moment," the story

00:00:22.839 --> 00:00:26.820
of a failed comedian whose character becomes
the face of global movement after a talk show

00:00:26.820 --> 00:00:27.540
outburst.

00:00:27.720 --> 00:00:31.900
The episode harks back to the very first Black
Mirror installment, "The National Anthem,"

00:00:31.909 --> 00:00:35.900
which was infamous for the scene in which
British Prime Minister Michael Callow has,

00:00:35.900 --> 00:00:40.430
um, intimate relations with a pig on live
television in order to save the life of a

00:00:40.430 --> 00:00:41.260
kidnapped royal.

00:00:41.560 --> 00:00:45.300
Naturally, both episodes feature a lot of
news reports, and "The Waldo Moment" marked

00:00:45.300 --> 00:00:49.720
the first time fictional network UKN reappeared
— the channel that beamed Pig-gate across

00:00:49.720 --> 00:00:51.160
the nation and around the world.

00:00:51.300 --> 00:00:55.440
Look a little closer, and you'll notice both
episodes also feature the same news bulletin

00:00:55.440 --> 00:00:59.300
declaring that an unrelated character has
been "cleared of wrongdoing following paparazzi

00:00:59.300 --> 00:00:59.800
scuffle."

00:01:00.100 --> 00:01:03.940
"The Waldo Moment" also contains an Easter
egg linking it with "Fifteen Million Merits,"

00:01:04.040 --> 00:01:07.780
which featured characters trying to escape
the life of an energy production drone by

00:01:07.780 --> 00:01:09.140
winning a talent competition.

00:01:09.520 --> 00:01:13.480
In that episode, contestant Abi Khan failed
to impress the judges with her vocals, but

00:01:13.490 --> 00:01:16.060
they did envision a future for in the adult
film industry.

00:01:16.060 --> 00:01:18.930
"So to be a singer, you gotta be truly exceptional."

00:01:18.930 --> 00:01:19.620
"Yeah."

00:01:19.740 --> 00:01:23.960
As a wink to that development, in "The Waldo
Moment," a digital billboard shot of her can

00:01:23.960 --> 00:01:25.640
be seen next to an image of Waldo.

00:01:26.340 --> 00:01:27.640
White Christmas

00:01:28.020 --> 00:01:32.240
A few months before Mad Men's final episodes
aired, actor Jon Hamm decided to hop from

00:01:32.240 --> 00:01:37.000
the '60s business world into the dystopian
future by way of Black Mirror's holiday episode,

00:01:37.100 --> 00:01:38.040
"White Christmas."

00:01:38.040 --> 00:01:42.280
In the episode, he plays a man with a much
more sordid past than Dick Whitman who's cooperating

00:01:42.280 --> 00:01:45.480
with police on another case to get his own
criminal sentence reduced.

00:01:45.620 --> 00:01:49.560
As he makes acquaintances with another character,
we get some smalltalk containing a wink to

00:01:49.569 --> 00:01:50.869
his star-making role.

00:01:50.869 --> 00:01:53.580
"My job was completely different.

00:01:55.340 --> 00:01:56.900
You'll never guess what that was."

00:01:59.680 --> 00:02:00.360
"Marketing?"

00:02:00.600 --> 00:02:03.100
And that wasn't the only interesting reference
of the episode.

00:02:03.110 --> 00:02:07.070
The other character flips past an advertisement
for the "Fifteen Million Merits" program Hot

00:02:07.070 --> 00:02:11.200
Shots, and there's a karaoke scene that features
the same song that Abi performed in the talent

00:02:11.200 --> 00:02:11.700
show.

00:02:11.840 --> 00:02:16.440
Meanwhile, one of Hamm's online cohorts goes
by the screen name Pie Ape, which was that

00:02:16.440 --> 00:02:19.460
episode's slang for a fat person — and another
is named Waldo.

00:02:19.820 --> 00:02:24.090
Meanwhile, fictional news network UKN makes
another comeback in the episode, this time

00:02:24.090 --> 00:02:28.000
to inform viewers that the court has rejected
an appeal of Victoria Skillane.

00:02:28.120 --> 00:02:31.959
You might remember her as the woman from Season
2's "White Bear" who has her memory wiped

00:02:31.960 --> 00:02:36.700
nightly and is forced to relive a single day
of torment indefinitely as punishment for

00:02:36.700 --> 00:02:37.800
her part in the demise of a child.

00:02:37.940 --> 00:02:41.500
"White Christmas" might have seemed like a
Santa-friendly episode, but the showrunners

00:02:41.500 --> 00:02:45.100
were clearly working with the Easter bunny
to make all these references.

00:02:45.440 --> 00:02:46.420
Nosedive

00:02:47.040 --> 00:02:51.800
Another nod to the disgraced Prime Minister
arrives in Season 3's premiere episode "Nosedive."

00:02:51.940 --> 00:02:56.600
The episode is a satire about a social media
climber who's terribly obsessed with the popularity

00:02:56.600 --> 00:02:57.280
of an old friend.

00:02:57.420 --> 00:03:01.560
As she checks her profile, we see a status
update from Michael Callow pop up, which reveals

00:03:01.560 --> 00:03:03.580
that he's "just gotten thrown out of the zoo
again."

00:03:03.740 --> 00:03:05.880
Looks like they don't trust him around livestock
anymore.

00:03:06.140 --> 00:03:10.000
Later, when Lacie is forced to ride a bus
with fans of a show called Sea of Tranquility,

00:03:10.019 --> 00:03:13.930
it's also a reminder of the inaugural episode,
because one of the show's special effects

00:03:13.930 --> 00:03:18.160
designers was hired by Callow's team to try
and come up with a digital swine solution.

00:03:18.920 --> 00:03:19.820
Playtest

00:03:20.200 --> 00:03:24.840
Season 3's "Playtest" centers on an American
backpacker who takes part in a test run of

00:03:24.840 --> 00:03:28.260
a new virtual reality horror game at a site
just outside of London.

00:03:28.520 --> 00:03:32.620
Cooper is given the inside track on the gaming
company by British hook-up Sonja, who's played

00:03:32.620 --> 00:03:34.480
by an actress from "Fifteen Million Merits."

00:03:34.620 --> 00:03:38.680
Later, the same symbol that appears on the
mask of the armed hunter throughout "White

00:03:38.689 --> 00:03:42.219
Bear" can be seen on one of the discs that
later turn to gopher holes as part of the

00:03:42.220 --> 00:03:43.880
whack-a-mole style warm-up game.

00:03:44.040 --> 00:03:48.800
Sonja also shows him a copy of EDGE magazine
with the gaming company's founder, Shou Saito,

00:03:48.800 --> 00:03:49.500
on the cover.

00:03:49.620 --> 00:03:53.140
Easter egg hunters were quick to point out
the block of text located in the bottom corner

00:03:53.140 --> 00:03:57.790
of the page that advertises an article about
Granular, the company behind the AI bees that

00:03:57.790 --> 00:04:00.739
run amok in the Season 3 finale, "Hated in
the Nation."

00:04:00.739 --> 00:04:04.390
Meanwhile, the words at the top of the page
refer to the virtual consciousness company

00:04:04.390 --> 00:04:05.580
TCKR.

00:04:06.180 --> 00:04:07.440
Shut Up and Dance

00:04:07.900 --> 00:04:11.860
Season 3's "Shut Up and Dance" followed the
plight of Kenny, who's coerced into committing

00:04:11.860 --> 00:04:14.140
various crimes by hackers who are blackmailing
him.

00:04:14.549 --> 00:04:18.440
While he's being threatened by text, you can
clearly see a sticker of our cartoon friend

00:04:18.440 --> 00:04:19.950
Waldo lurking on his desk.

00:04:19.950 --> 00:04:23.940
And when another target of the hackers opens
a webpage to a story about her leaked private

00:04:24.000 --> 00:04:27.480
emails, you can see several callbacks to prior
episodes on-screen.

00:04:27.700 --> 00:04:31.740
First, there's the shamed former Prime Minister
from the series premiere in the side column,

00:04:31.760 --> 00:04:32.900
announcing his divorce.

00:04:33.060 --> 00:04:36.660
And just below him is a courtroom sketch of
Victoria Skillane from Season 2.

00:04:36.840 --> 00:04:40.960
Above that, there's also an ad for "One Smart
Cookie" and the "kitchen tech of tomorrow,"

00:04:40.970 --> 00:04:44.750
is a clear callback to the "White Christmas"
special where computer-generated versions

00:04:44.750 --> 00:04:47.000
of people are created to serve their host's
every need.

00:04:47.160 --> 00:04:50.980
And above that there's a mention of the talent
competition from "15 Million Merits."

00:04:50.980 --> 00:04:54.540
Last but not least, there's also a familiar
bank logo in the episode.

00:04:54.620 --> 00:04:58.240
The bank Kenny robs appears to be a branch
of the same company that failed to dispense

00:04:58.250 --> 00:05:00.110
Cooper's flight money in "Playtest."

00:05:00.110 --> 00:05:03.830
Perhaps more interestingly, the logo also
greatly resembles the one that appears on

00:05:03.830 --> 00:05:07.699
the military uniforms and equipment in "Men
Against Fire," which is a pretty deep piece

00:05:07.700 --> 00:05:10.270
of commentary on the profitability of war.

00:05:10.560 --> 00:05:11.640
Hated in the Nation

00:05:12.160 --> 00:05:16.560
During the episode "Hated in the Nation,"
policewoman Karin Parke watches a UKN news

00:05:16.569 --> 00:05:20.530
report, and along the bottom of the screen
runs a headline announcing the US military's

00:05:20.530 --> 00:05:24.440
approval of the MASS Project, the implants
that allow the soldiers to take out targets

00:05:24.440 --> 00:05:26.199
in cold blood in "Men Against Fire".

00:05:26.199 --> 00:05:30.240
It also tells us that cookies have finally
been given human rights protections, which

00:05:30.240 --> 00:05:33.480
is welcome news after everything that went
down in "White Christmas."

00:05:33.600 --> 00:05:37.920
Meanwhile, the story centers on a madman's
plot to use artificially intelligent pollination

00:05:37.930 --> 00:05:41.560
bees to assassinate people of the public's
hashtag choosing — and who else do they

00:05:41.560 --> 00:05:43.820
want eliminated but Michael Callow himself.

00:05:44.140 --> 00:05:47.860
Meanwhile, some people are pleading for Victoria
Skillane's freedom, while others want her

00:05:47.860 --> 00:05:48.360
in the ground.

00:05:48.460 --> 00:05:53.120
Interestingly, it turns out Karen's new partner
Blue Coulson was instrumental in taking down

00:05:53.140 --> 00:05:54.900
Skillane's boyfriend, Ian Rannoch.

00:05:55.120 --> 00:05:59.900
The news also mentions that the gaming company
from "Playtest" is revealed to be under investigation

00:05:59.900 --> 00:06:01.400
over the disappearance of a tourist.

00:06:01.400 --> 00:06:04.040
That's a clear reference to our poor pal Cooper.

00:06:04.100 --> 00:06:08.280
And Victoria Skillane has attempted her end
her own life after continuing to be incarcerated

00:06:08.280 --> 00:06:08.880
at White Bear.

00:06:09.300 --> 00:06:09.800
Yikes.

00:06:09.960 --> 00:06:13.700
Here's hoping Season 4 is as loaded with callbacks
as what we've seen so far!

00:06:14.060 --> 00:06:14.820
Thanks for watching!

00:06:15.100 --> 00:06:17.400
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:06:17.580 --> 00:06:19.720
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

